  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 130 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nDrier and warmer conditions continue on Wednesday as heights rise \nslightly behind the shortwave that is expected to pass overhead \ntoday. However, the broad upper-level trough across the western \nCONUS deepens, putting our area under southwest flow aloft. \nSeveral shortwaves moving within the flow yield medium to high (40-\n80%) chances of showers and thunderstorms early Thursday morning \nthrough Thursday night. Forecast soundings per ensemble guidance \ncontinues to depict PWAT values in the 1.0\"-1.5\" range (> 90th \npercentile), particularly across the eastern half of our CWA. This \nsuggests a favorable environment for potentially heavy rainfall that \ncould lead to localized flash flooding. However, details remain \nunclear at this time given uncertainty in where the best lift and \nmoisture quality will setup. \n\nBy Friday, southwesterly flow aloft remains the dominant \nfeature through at least Saturday, but the bulk of shortwave \ndisturbances begin to move east. This maintains a low (10-40%) \nchance of showers and thunderstorms across the forecast area during \nthis timeframe. The assistance of surface troughing and upslope flow \nyield locally higher probabilities (40-60%) over parts of the higher \nterrain Friday night. Long-range guidance shows the upper-level \npattern changing to quasi-zonal flow aloft, but a low (10-20%) \nchance of rain remains in the forecast across our northern zones for \nnow.  \n  \nGreening",
